{"tool": "read_file", "path": "loop/acceptance.md", "response": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"total_lines\": 42, \"text\": \"1: # Acceptance contract and provenance\\n2: \\n3: This document was added during the November 4, 2026 readiness work, after the\\n4: port existed. It consolidates existing checks and adds loop verification; it\\n5: is not evidence that criteria were specified before implementation. Existing\\n6: browser/audio acceptance checks are unchanged. Changes to those checks remain\\n7: human-owned and must be separately justified in artifacts/.\\n8: \\n9: ## Automated port scope\\n10: \\n11: Emscripten 4.0.18 must build the supplied engine and shareware WAD. The existing\\n12: `tests/test_browser.py`, `tests/browser.mjs`, and `tests/test_audio.py` must pass:\\n13: WAD/Wasm loading, visible framebuffer, new-game/menu navigation, ongoing\\n14: simulation, movement, turning, ammunition consumed by firing, nonzero effects\\n15: and music samples, volume/mute, use-key delivery/release, synthetic blur release,\\n16: alternate movement keys, weapons, automap and pause/resume. Fatal browser/network\\n17: errors fail the run. The music parser must accept all 13 scores, reject malformed\\n18: or truncated data and defer audio initialization until a gesture.\\n19: \\n20: A role PASS concerns this automated scope, not the whole assignment. Actual\\n21: door/switch activation, OS focus transitions, physical speakers, musical fidelity,\\n22: save persistence and mouse aiming are not established by these checks. A manual\\n23: playthrough remains separate evidence. Do not invent it.\\n24: \\n25: ## Loop scope\\n26: \\n27: Separate fresh coder, reviewer and tester processes exchange immutable MCP\\n28: handoffs. Only coder port-source writes are exposed. Protected tests, loop,\\n29: engine, WAD and executable build scripts cannot be edited through agent tools.\\n30: Tester must execute a fresh fixed verification and tie PASS to its source hash.\\n31: Every configured task must pass; stale completion is invalidated by source changes.\\n32: Interruptions preserve attempts and reserve unknown cost. Budgets and failure\\n33: statuses must be explicit. `tests/test_loop.py` exercises these invariants with\\n34: fixtures; it is not evidence of real autonomous repair or live resumption.\\n35: \\n36: ## Full assignment scope\\n37: \\n38: Independent verification, fault detection, actual autonomous repair of two blind\\n39: teammate/instructor regressions, resumption, accountability, design rationale and\\n40: reflection are separate requirements. The user explicitly requested omission of\\n41: the teammate exercise. Mark it NOT PERFORMED; never convert scoped SUCCESS into\\n42: full capstone acceptance. Historical token/cost usage remains unknown.\"}"}], "isError": false}}
{"tool": "read_file", "path": "platform/README.md", "response": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"total_lines\": 37, \"text\": \"1: # Browser port starting points\\n2: \\n3: `doomgeneric_wasm.c` deliberately contains empty platform callbacks and no\\n4: entry point. The engine is in `third_party/doomgeneric/`; its public platform\\n5: interface is `doomgeneric.h`. Build scripts and browser integration are student\\n6: work. The supplied HTML does not yet load a compiled module.\\n7: \\n8: Some details of this engine matter when implementing the browser port:\\n9: \\n10: - **Pixels:** the default framebuffer is 640 by 400, with 32-bit pixels.\\n11:   Despite the `rgba8888` mode name, `i_video.c` packs red at bit 16, green at\\n12:   bit 8, and blue at bit 0, without setting alpha. On little-endian WebAssembly\\n13:   the bytes are B, G, R, 0. This is not directly usable as opaque RGBA canvas\\n14:   `ImageData`.\\n15: - **Timing:** `doomgeneric_Tick()` is callable, but screen wipes in `d_main.c`\\n16:   and tic synchronization in `d_loop.c` still contain clock/sleep waits.\\n17:   Browser integration must account for those waits and allow the browser to\\n18:   process events. The stub clock always returning zero cannot run the game.\\n19: - **Input:** the interface accepts Doom key codes and press/release events.\\n20:   Mouse event generation is commented out in the supplied `i_input.c`.\\n21: - **Files:** the engine reads the WAD with standard C file operations.\\n22:   Keeping `assets/doom1.wad` on the host does not itself make that file\\n23:   available inside the browser module. Packaging and runtime paths are part\\n24:   of the port. Save/config persistence across reloads is also separate work.\\n25: - **Audio:** sound is disabled by default. Enabling `FEATURE_SOUND` requires\\n26:   implementations of `DG_sound_module` and `DG_music_module` from `i_sound.h`;\\n27:   it also activates an `SDL_mixer.h` include in `i_sound.c`. The six callbacks\\n28:   in `doomgeneric.h` do not implement audio. A browser-native backend must\\n29:   account for that include if it does not use SDL mixer.\\n30: \\n31: No additional sound assets are needed: the bundled WAD contains 55 sampled\\n32: sound effects, 13 MUS music scores, and GENMIDI instrument definitions.\\n33: Browser Web Audio can play decoded samples without external audio libraries.\\n34: Music needs sequencing and synthesis; the included `mus2mid.c` converts score\\n35: data but does not generate audio. Reproducing OPL-style instruments is a\\n36: larger task than playing sound effects. See the\\n37: [Web Audio specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/webaudio-1.0/).\"}"}], "isError": false}}
